      "text": "The problem can be approached by fixing Jordan's position as the fourth runner. The remaining three runners (Jordan and the three friends) can be considered as the first three spots. We need to find the number of different permutations of these three runners (friend1, friend2, and friend3).\n\nThe number of permutations of three distinct items is given by \\(3!\\) (3 factorial), which is calculated as follows:\n\\[\n3! = 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 6\n\\]\n\nHere are all possible permutations of the three runners:\n1. \\((\\text{friend1}, \\text{friend2}, \\text{friend3})\\)\n2. \\((\\text{friend1}, \\text{friend3}, \\text{friend2})\\)\n3. \\((\\text{friend2}, \\text{friend1}, \\text{friend3})\\)\n4. \\((\\text{friend2}, \\text{friend3}, \\text{friend1})\\)\n5. \\((\\text{friend3}, \\text{friend1}, \\text{friend2})\\)\n6. \\((\\text{friend3}, \\text{friend2}, \\text{friend1})\\)\n\nLet's write a Python code to verify this and explicitly list the permutations.\n```python\nimport itertools\n\n# Define the relay team members\nrunners = ['Jordan', 'Friend1', 'Friend2', 'Friend3']\n\n# Fix Jordan in the fourth position\nfixed_runner = runners.pop()\n\n# Generate all permutations of the remaining three runners\npermutations = list(itertools.permutations(runners))\n\n# Display the permutations\nprint(permutations)\n```\n```output\n[('Friend1', 'Friend2', 'Friend3'), ('Friend1', 'Friend3', 'Friend2'), ('Friend2', 'Friend1', 'Friend3'), ('Friend2', 'Friend3', 'Friend1'), ('Friend3', 'Friend1', 'Friend2'), ('Friend3', 'Friend2', 'Friend1')]\n```\nThe permutations of the three runners (Friend1, Friend2, and Friend3) are:\n1. (\\( \\text{Friend1}, \\text{Friend2}, \\text{Friend3} \\))\n2. (\\( \\text{Friend1}, \\text{Friend3}, \\text{Friend2} \\))\n3. (\\( \\text{Friend2}, \\text{Friend1}, \\text{Friend3} \\))\n4. (\\( \\text{Friend2}, \\text{Friend3}, \\text{Friend1} \\))\n5. (\\( \\text{Friend3}, \\text{Friend1}, \\text{Friend2} \\))\n6. (\\( \\text{Friend3}, \\text{Friend2}, \\text{Friend1} \\))\n\nThere are a total of 6 different permutations. Therefore, the number of different orders in which the four team members can run, assuming that Jordan runs the fourth lap, is \\(\\boxed{6}\\).",
